schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Cooperative types (worker / consumer / savings-and-loan / multi-stakeholder)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Four cooperative models the article distinguishes as building blocks for
    thinking about what might work locally — owned/controlled by workers, by
    consumers pooling purchasing power, by savers/lenders (credit unions), or
    jointly by multiple stakeholder groups.
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used as a taxonomy readers can map onto existing informal practice
    (savings circles, burial societies) before deciding what to formalize.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - credit-union (the savings-and-loan cooperative form, "one of the most widespread cooperative forms in Africa" per article)
    - community-supported-agriculture (named as the consumer-cooperative example)
  important_distinctions:
    - "a cooperative differs from a company in that the people who do the work/use the service own and govern it, rather than outside investors capturing profits"
  tensions:
    - single-stakeholder-simplicity vs multi-stakeholder-representativeness
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Presenting these as four clean categories understates that real-world
    cooperatives (e.g. Somaliland's informal savings circles) often blend
    features and predate formal legal typologies.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/cooperative-commons.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-d800b62ea134
  source_lineage: cooperative-commons.md
